---
id: joins-01
courseId: fullstack
title: "Why your ==JOIN== inflated the revenue"
concepts: [inner-join, row-multiplication]
---

{% hook id="h1" estSeconds=40 character="bug" concepts=["row-multiplication"] %}
Friday, 18:40. Finance says revenue is ==four times== too high. I may have helped.
{% margin %}
{% stop %}
What could make one number show up four times?
{% /stop %}
{% /margin %}
{% /hook %}

{% predict id="p1" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/join.sql" language="sql" concepts=["inner-join", "row-multiplication"] %}
{% option output="100" misconception="join-keeps-row-count" %}
There are 100 orders, so the join must give 100 rows.
{% /option %}
{% option output="320" correct=true %}
Each order has four items, so every order with items shows up four times. Orders 81 to 100 have no items and vanish.
{% /option %}
{% option output="420" misconception="join-is-union" %}
A join pairs rows side by side. It does not stack the two tables.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
Wait, doesn't a join just add more columns?
{% /sticky %}
{% diagram ref="one-order-four-items" caption="one order, four items..." /%}
{% gotcha %}
A join never asks how many orders you have. It counts matching pairs.
{% /gotcha %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e1" estSeconds=45 code="select o.id, i.sku\nfrom orders o\njoin items i on i.order_id = o.id" concepts=["inner-join"] %}
**What really happened:** a join returns one row for every matching pair. Order 7 has four items, so it appears four times, and its amount appears four times too. An order with no items gives none.

{% annotation line=3 %}
Each matching item adds another row.
{% /annotation %}
{% margin %}
{% diagram ref="orders-x-items" caption="follow order 7" /%}
{% diagram ref="row-multiplication" caption="one becomes four" /%}
{% gotcha %}
**If you picked 100:** a join lists matching pairs, it does not add columns. **If you picked 420:** it pairs rows, it never stacks tables.
{% /gotcha %}
{% /margin %}
{% /explain %}

{% predict id="p2" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/sum.sql" language="sql" concepts=["row-multiplication"] %}
{% option output="1600" misconception="join-keeps-row-count" %}
That is the true revenue: 80 paid orders of 20. The join does not keep each order once.
{% /option %}
{% option output="6400" correct=true %}
Each order amount is added once per item, so 80 orders of 20 become 320 rows of 20. This is the report's bug.
{% /option %}
{% option output="1920" misconception="join-counts-orders" %}
The sum does not count orders or items. It adds the amount on every row the join produced.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% stop %}
How many times is each order's amount added to the sum?
{% /stop %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% fillBlanks id="f1" estSeconds=60 template="select count(___fn___ o.id)\nfrom orders o\njoin items i on i.order_id = o.id" language="sql" concepts=["inner-join"] %}
{% blank id="fn" accepted=["distinct"] misconception="join-counts-orders" %}
distinct makes each order count once, however many items it has.
{% /blank %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="says" %}
Oh. So the join didn't lie. My sum did.
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% predict id="p3" estSeconds=60 code="select count(distinct o.id)\nfrom orders o\njoin items i on i.order_id = o.id" language="sql" concepts=["row-multiplication"] %}
{% option output="100" misconception="join-keeps-row-count" %}
The join drops the 20 orders without items, so not all 100 orders are counted.
{% /option %}
{% option output="320" misconception="join-counts-orders" %}
That is the number of rows. distinct counts each order once.
{% /option %}
{% option output="80" correct=true %}
Distinct order ids among the joined rows: only the 80 orders that have items.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% sqlLab id="s1" estSeconds=120 prompt="Return one row with one column named n: the number of orders that have at least one item." seedRef="default" starter="select count(*) as n from orders" solution="select count(distinct order_id) as n from items" concepts=["row-multiplication"] %}
{% hint %}
The starter counts every order, including empty carts. Which table only has orders that have items?
{% /hint %}
{% hint %}
Count the order ids in items, but each order id must be counted only once.
{% /hint %}
{% hint %}
Use count with distinct on the column order_id, and read from items.
{% /hint %}
{% margin %}
{% gotcha %}
A plain count after a join counts matching pairs, not orders.
{% /gotcha %}
{% /margin %}
{% /sqlLab %}

{% pitfall id="pf1" estSeconds=40 language="sql" badCode="select sum(o.amount)\nfrom orders o\njoin items i on i.order_id = o.id" goodCode="select sum(amount)\nfrom orders" concepts=["row-multiplication"] %}
Summing a column of the one side after a join to the many side adds it once per match. Sum at the grain of that table, or aggregate the many side before you join.
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Summing after the join is my favorite mistake. Don't tell finance.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /pitfall %}

{% sqlLab id="s2" estSeconds=90 prompt="Return one row with one column named revenue: the true total amount of all orders." seedRef="default" starter="select sum(o.amount) as revenue from orders o join items i on i.order_id = o.id" solution="select sum(amount) as revenue from orders" concepts=["row-multiplication"] %}
{% hint %}
The amount lives in orders. Do you need items to add it up?
{% /hint %}
{% hint %}
Remove the join. Each order should be added once.
{% /hint %}
{% hint %}
Sum the amount column, reading only from orders.
{% /hint %}
{% /sqlLab %}

{% fillBlanks id="f2" estSeconds=60 template="select o.id, count(i.id) as items\nfrom orders o\njoin items i on i.order_id = o.id\ngroup by ___col___" language="sql" concepts=["inner-join"] %}
{% blank id="col" accepted=["o.id", "1"] misconception="join-keeps-row-count" %}
Group by the order id to get one row per order again.
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% recap id="rc1" estSeconds=30 concepts=["inner-join", "row-multiplication"] %}
{% point %}
A join returns ==one row per matching pair==.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
One left row with many matches appears many times.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
Count or sum at the right grain: use distinct or aggregate before you join.
{% /point %}
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Fine. Revenue is correct again. I'll find something else.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /recap %}

{% predict id="p4" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/left.sql" language="sql" concepts=["inner-join"] %}
{% option output="100" misconception="join-keeps-row-count" %}
Orders still repeat once per item, so the count is not 100.
{% /option %}
{% option output="340" correct=true %}
320 matched rows, plus one row each for the 20 orders without items. A left join keeps them.
{% /option %}
{% option output="420" misconception="join-is-union" %}
A join does not stack 100 orders on top of 320 items.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% cliffhanger id="c1" estSeconds=20 nextLessonId="query-order-01" question="You write select first, but the database does not start there. Where does it start, and why does that break an alias in where?" %}
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Next lesson I'm hiding in your alias. Good luck finding me.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /cliffhanger %}
